    "body": "2026 SEMA Show • 2028 Mitsubishi Outlander Diamond Trail Edition: How Does It Compare to Other Adventure Crossovers?\n2026 and 2027 Nissan Kicks Earn Top Safety Pick Accolade From the IIHS\n23 photos\nPhoto: IIHS on YouTube / edited\nRedesigned in 2024 for model year 2025, the second generation of the Nissan Kicks needed two attempts to get its rear occupant crash protection right. At the start of the 2026 model year, the Japanese automaker made targeted changes to the rear seatbelt load limiters. Further changes were implemented for crossovers produced after June 2026, helping the Kicks ace the moderate overlap crash test.\nEarlier 2026 models retain the previous \"marginal\" rating. After June 2026, the Kicks received select rear seat and seatbelt load limiter changes designed to improve rear occupant safety in this exact crash scenario. Customers who are curious when their Kicks was produced are presented with this information on the certification label, which is affixed either on the driver door or the B-pillar.\nRather than \"marginal,\" the 2025 Nissan Kicks scored \"poor\" in said test. Before going any further, it's necessary for us to highlight what load limiters actually do. In the case of automotive seatbelts, these limiters are designed to control how much webbing is released under specific crash forces. They basically manage the force the seatbelt places on the occupant's chest.\nThe latest IIHS moderate overlap test involving the Kicks involved a 2026 model year SV with all-wheel drive, and its result carries over to the 2027 model. Up front, the dummy in the driver's seat received \"good\" ratings across the board, including for neck, thighs, and lower leg protection.\nPhoto: IIHS on YouTube Out back, the rear passenger scored \"acceptable\" ratings for chest protection, restraints, and kinematics. Everything else is listed as green by the nonprofit organization, and the rest of the vehicle has a fairly good report card as well. Gifted with the Top Safety Pick accolade, models produced after June 2026 are rated \"good\" in the other two crashworthiness tests, for headlight performance, pedestrian front crash prevention, and their seatbelt reminders.\nEven the LATCH system's ease of use is listed as \"G+,\" but alas, the IIHS could not give the Kicks the Top Safety Pick+ award due to vehicle-to-vehicle front crash prevention. \"Marginal\" is the overall result in this regard, with the AEB system having trouble identifying motorcycles in the 31-mph centered test.\nSlotting well below the Rogue and Leaf crossovers, the Kicks is now configurable stateside from $22,790 sans destination. Based on the high-spec version of the CMF-B platform, which makes the Kicks related to the Nissan Juke, this fellow is presently advertised with $2,000 retail holiday cash by Nissan North America.\nSaid offer applies to SR customers, namely those who purchase SRs from new dealer stock. The SR slots below the range-topping Rock Creek , with the Rock Creek boasting all-wheel drive by default. Lesser trim levels include the SV and the base S, with the S being offered with front-wheel drive exclusively.\nAll trims feature a 2.0-liter naturally aspirated I4 with 141 horsepower on tap, which is connected exclusively to an Xtronic continuously variable tranny. As opposed to other grades, the Rock Creek uses a specific torque-vectoring algo that proactively routes torque to the rear wheels the moment it senses loose terrain. Even no, there are no gearing or final drive ratio differences between it and other trim levels.\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more articles from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\nNissan Kicks 2027 nissan kicks 2026 Nissan Kicks Nissan safety\nAbout the author: Mircea Panait\nAfter a 1:43 scale model of a Ferrari 250 GTO sparked Mircea's interest for cars when he was a kid, an early internship at Top Gear sealed his career path. He's most interested in muscle cars and American trucks, but he takes a passing interest in quirky kei cars as well. Full profile\nUP NEXT DDPAI Drops Their Latest Z100 Flagship Dash Cam and They've Outdone Themselves – Starts at $169",
